Done. `app.js:4-9` is the only change:

```js
function login(username, password) {
  console.log("Logging in:", username);
  // Stub: would POST to API_ENDPOINT and read userId from the response.
  const userId = `stub-${username}`;
  console.log("Logged in userId:", userId);
  return { success: true, user: username, userId };
}
```

`index.html` and the submit handler are untouched — the handler's existing `console.log("Login result:", result)` now carries `userId` along with the rest of the object.

**Verification.** `node --check app.js` passes. I also evaluated the file under a minimal `document` stub and called `login("alice", "pw")` directly; it printed `Logged in userId: stub-alice` and returned `{"success":true,"user":"alice","userId":"stub-alice"}`. I did not open `index.html` in a browser, so the form-submit path itself is unverified end to end — the handler code is unchanged, but that's inference, not an observation.

The `stub-` prefix is deliberately obvious so it can't pass for a real identifier; replacing it with the API response is the single line that changes when the POST gets wired up.
